From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: tiled present: only the changed part of the window goes to WindowServer

What: with OMACVM_GL_PRESENT_TILES=1 (for tests: the app gets a switch once
the VM side sends its damage, #520) the view's layer keeps the last whole
frame and a grid of 256x256 tile layers
lies over it (ui/omacvm-present-tiles.h). A guest frame whose changed box
(the guest's flush rects since the last frame) covers at most 12 tiles is
drawn into those tiles' own small surfaces only and put on their layers;
WindowServer then composites only them. Each tile is drawn with the
viewport inside its own surface and its part of the frame picked by
texture coordinates (the tile program, made where the switch is read;
tiles stay off when it does not build). A frame with nothing changed is not
presented. Any other frame is whole, as before, and hides the tiles: the
boot logo, extra brightness, another size or colour path, a scanout buffer
not seen lately or not flushed yet, and more than 12 tiles. Each whole
frame shown measures if the window shows that size pixel for pixel; if
not (a window that scales the picture), frames stay whole until it does
again, so they never go back and forth. Frames skipped in the present
queue hand their tiles to the frame after them. Only with the vsync
present. Each tile keeps five surfaces while it is used (at most 32
tiles; none 10 s after the last frame in tiles). =check also compares
every 30th tile frame's tiles with the same frame drawn whole at their
own precision (qemu.log "tiles check"); qemu.log says once a minute how
many frames went in tiles (and, with =check, how many checks differed).

Why: WindowServer composites the whole window again for every new whole
surface, also when only the pointer moved. MacBook Air M2, the VM full
screen at 60 Hz with a guest that sends its damage (#520's driver), the
pointer moving: 3.13-3.17 W instead of 3.55-3.75 W for the whole Mac (two
sets), WindowServer 43 instead of 155 GPU ms/s, the same frame rate,
pictures identical.

The guest must say what changed: Linux before 7.4 flushes the whole plane
on every page flip (the frame-path work, #520, backports the fix), and then
every frame is whole, as without it.

Tiles are never drawn with a viewport that starts outside their surface:
Apple's OpenGL (4.1 on Metal) squeezes a viewport that starts more than
4096 pixels outside its target (its start becomes -4096, its far edge
stays). 3.2.14 drew each tile as the frame's quad moved by -x, -y, so on
frames wider than 4352 pixels the tiles right of x 4096 came out squeezed
(5120 pixels wide: the last three columns, 0.95, 0.90 and 0.85) until a
whole frame hid them: the right part flickered while the pointer moved
there (ADR 0055).

Upstream: QEMU's cocoa display has no IOSurface present; not sent.
Test: Tests/display/test-present-tiles.sh (the rules; the tile shader
against whole frames on Apple's software renderer, test-present-tiles-gl.m,
--gpu on the GPU; the wiring in the patched ui/cocoa.m; --self-test: every
check fails on the code without it, the runtime build runs it on the real
cocoa.m before this patch).
Applies after omacvm-cocoa-present-lossless.patch (it uses the partial
present's rects, rules and checks), before omacvm-cocoa-no-app-nap.patch.
